16:41:59 said  I built **Quickfind**, a file-name search tool modelled on the original. It indexes every drive on this computer: it found about 3.2 million files and folders, and after the first scan it opens with everything listed in about one and a half seconds. Typing searches as you go, with most searches taking under a tenth of a second. It keeps up with files you add, rename or delete while it's open.
16:41:59 said  **What matches the original:**
16:41:59 said  - **Search language:** it uses the same rules I read out of the original's code. That covers AND, OR (`|`), NOT (`!`) and `< >` grouping, wildcards, `case:`, `path:`, `ext:`, `size:` (with the original's tiny, small and huge size bands), dates like `dm:today`, and `dupe:`.
16:41:59 said  - **Filters:** Audio, Document, Picture and the rest use the original's exact file-type lists.
16:41:59 said  - **Window:** same menus, Name / Path / Size / Date Modified columns, a small arrow on the sorted column, and the "N objects" status bar.
16:41:59 said  - **Other features:** bookmarks, exporting results to a file, and the original's start-up options for searching, filtering and sorting.
16:41:59 said  **What differs or is still unknown:**
16:41:59 said  - **Columns:** the Name and Path columns are a bit narrower than the original's, so Size and Date fit in the window without scrolling sideways.
16:41:59 said  - **Size format:** sizes show as KB rounded up, which is a guess. The original never finished loading its own file list while I watched it, so I couldn't see its Size column.
16:41:59 said  - **How it reads the drives:** the original reads the disk's file table directly, which is faster but usually needs administrator rights. Mine does a normal folder scan each time it starts instead.
16:41:59 said  - **Not included:** searching inside file contents, music and photo details, and the original's network-sharing servers.
16:41:59 said  - **Memory:** it uses about 400–480 MB of memory, which is more than the original.
16:41:59 said  To make it build, I installed a second, self-contained version of Rust's build tools, because this computer has no Microsoft C++ tools. I also had to hold a few libraries at older versions. The automatic check passed the build and the screenshot. It can't yet measure whether the app really behaves by the rules sheet.
16:42:00 said  A good next request would be an Options window: remembering your column choices, hiding system or hidden files, and leaving out folders you never want searched.
